### Definition of LinkedHashMap & The Dual-Mode Architecture

A **`java.util.LinkedHashMap<K, V>`** is a hash table and doubly-linked list implementation of the `Map` interface. It inherits all hashing mechanisms from `HashMap` while threading a doubly-linked list through all its entries.

It operates in one of two modes, determined by the `accessOrder` constructor parameter:
```java
public LinkedHashMap(int initialCapacity, float loadFactor, boolean accessOrder)
```
1. **Insertion-Order (`accessOrder = false`, Default)**:
   * Entries are visited in the order they were inserted.
   * Calling `get()` or re-inserting an existing key does not modify the sequence.
2. **Access-Order (`accessOrder = true`)**:
   * Entries are visited from **least-recently accessed** to **most-recently accessed**.
   * Any call to `get()`, `put()`, or `getOrDefault()` moves the accessed entry to the tail (end) of the doubly-linked list.

---

### Building an In-Memory LRU Cache with removeEldestEntry()

`LinkedHashMap` provides a built-in eviction hook invoked by `put()` and `putAll()`:
```java
protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K,V> eldest) {
    return false; // Default: Never removes eldest entry
}
```
By subclassing `LinkedHashMap` and overriding this method to return `size() > maxCapacity`, the map automatically deletes the least-recently used entry (the head) as soon as capacity is exceeded!

---

### Executable Code Walkthrough: Production LRU Cache

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LruCacheDemo {
    public static class SimpleLruCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {
        private final int maxCapacity;

        public SimpleLruCache(int maxCapacity) {
            // initialCapacity, loadFactor, accessOrder = true
            super(maxCapacity, 0.75f, true);
            this.maxCapacity = maxCapacity;
        }

        @Override
        protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
            return size() > maxCapacity; // Evict eldest when full!
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Cache with max capacity 3
        SimpleLruCache<String, String> cache = new SimpleLruCache<>(3);

        cache.put("Page-1", "Home");
        cache.put("Page-2", "About");
        cache.put("Page-3", "Contact");
        System.out.println("1. Initial Cache: " + cache.keySet());

        // Access Page-1 (moves Page-1 to the most-recently-used tail!)
        cache.get("Page-1");
        System.out.println("2. After accessing Page-1: " + cache.keySet());

        // Insert Page-4: exceeds capacity 3!
        // Page-2 (the least recently used) is automatically evicted!
        cache.put("Page-4", "Dashboard");
        System.out.println("3. After adding Page-4: " + cache.keySet());
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
1. Initial Cache: [Page-1, Page-2, Page-3]
2. After accessing Page-1: [Page-2, Page-3, Page-1]
3. After adding Page-4: [Page-3, Page-1, Page-4]
```

---

### Comparison: Insertion-Order vs Access-Order

| Attribute | Insertion-Order (`accessOrder = false`) | Access-Order (`accessOrder = true`) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`get(key)` Side Effect** | None (read-only) | Mutates entry pointer to tail |
| **Tail Element** | Most recently inserted key | Most recently accessed or inserted key |
| **Head Element** | First inserted key | Least recently accessed key (LRU candidate) |
| **Primary Use Case** | Predictable JSON serialization, audit logs | In-memory caching, buffer pools, session expiry |

